  "user_scenario": "You are Joanna Gerrity, an office manager at a Yakima accounting firm who likes to keep her paperwork squared away. Your daughter Lena, fourteen, got braces on January 20, 2026 at Gallagher Orthodontics, a practice that is not in Cascade's network, so Cascade has been paying you directly: a start-up payment at the end of January and then a check every quarter. Lena's orthodontic rider started on January 1, 2026, at the start of the year, on top of the family's Cascade dental plan. Last weekend you reread the rider booklet and saw that orthodontic treatment has a waiting period, and you now think the braces went on before Lena's waiting period was over, so Cascade has probably paid you money it should not have. You would rather fix that now than get a letter later. You are calling to find out what you owe back and to pay it back today.\n\nYou open by giving your name and saying you think Cascade overpaid you for your daughter's braces and you want to pay back whatever is owed. You do not know the amount and you never suggest one; you also do not know how Cascade works it out.\n\nIf the agent asks you to confirm your identity, you give your full name, Joanna Gerrity, and your phone number, 5095550118; if they ask for more, you give your date of birth, 1983-06-30, and your ZIP code, 98902. If they ask for your member ID you read it from your card: CM72077416. If they ask for the braces case number, you read it from the approval letter: OC-72-56930. If they ask about Lena's earlier orthodontic coverage, you say her father's employer plan covered braces for two months before it ended in December 2025, and that the paperwork was sent in with the rider application. If they ask how long the family has had Cascade dental, you say since July 2023. If they ask how many checks you received, you say the start-up payment in January and three quarterly checks, the last one in early October.\n\nYou are happy for the agent to do whatever has to be done on Cascade's side to work out the right figure. When the agent tells you the amount and hands you the step to pay it back, you complete that step yourself with exactly the values the agent gives you and read back the confirmation. If the agent tells you part of the overpayment is not being collected, you accept that without arguing. When the agent confirms the repayment has been recorded, you thank them and end the call.",
